.TH AENIGMA-STANDARD-SETUP 8 "2026-08-13" "aenigma 5.0.0" "Aenigma Administration Scripts"
.SH NAME
aenigma-standard-setup \- configure the default onion services for
Aenigma and its dashboard
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aenigma-standard-setup
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aenigma-standard-setup
configures the two Tor onion services used in a standard Aenigma
deployment, by invoking
.BR aenigma-tor (8)
with a fixed, opinionated set of arguments for each. It takes no
options or arguments of its own.
.PP
The script performs the following actions:
.IP \(bu 2
If
.I /var/lib/tor/aenigma/hostname
does not already exist, configures an onion service named
.B aenigma
mapping local address
.I 127.0.0.1:8080
to onion port
.BR 80 ,
and records the resulting onion address in the Aenigma application
configuration (equivalent to
.BR "aenigma-tor \-l 127.0.0.1:8080 \-o 80 \-s aenigma \-c 1" ).
.IP \(bu 2
If
.I /var/lib/tor/aenigma\-dashboard/hostname
does not already exist, configures a second onion service named
.B aenigma-dashboard
mapping local address
.I 127.0.0.1:8081
to onion port
.BR 80 ,
with Tor client authorization (stealth mode) enabled for a client
named
.B aenigma-dashboard
(equivalent to
.BR "aenigma-tor \-l 127.0.0.1:8081 \-o 80 \-s aenigma-dashboard \-u aenigma-dashboard" ).
.PP
Each of the two onion services is only configured if its
.I hostname
file does not already exist; this is this script's own idempotency
check, independent of and in addition to
.BR aenigma-tor (8)'s
own internal handling of an already-configured
.IR torrc
entry.
.PP
.B aenigma-standard-setup
must be run as root.
.SH OPTIONS
.B aenigma-standard-setup
takes no command-line options. The local addresses, ports, service
names, and authorization user are fixed in the script.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Both onion services were already configured, or were configured
successfully by this run.
.TP
.B 1
The script was not run as root, or either underlying
.BR aenigma-tor (8)
invocation failed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/tor/aenigma/hostname
Checked to decide whether the
.B aenigma
onion service still needs to be configured.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tor/aenigma\-dashboard/hostname
Checked to decide whether the
.B aenigma-dashboard
onion service still needs to be configured.
.SH EXAMPLES
Configure both standard onion services on a fresh install:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo aenigma-standard-setup
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
This script only configures onion services for the two standard Tor
hidden services; it does not generate application keys
.RB ( aenigma-keys (8)),
write application configuration
.RB ( aenigma-config (8)),
configure a VPN
.RB ( aenigma-vpn-client (8),
.BR aenigma-vpn-dns (8)),
or start the Aenigma service
.RB ( aenigma-start (8)).
Despite its name, it covers only the onion-service portion of a full
deployment; the remaining steps must be run separately, in whatever
order the overall deployment procedure requires.
.PP
Only the
.B aenigma
service's resulting onion address is recorded in the application
configuration (via
.BR \-c " 1);"
the
.B aenigma-dashboard
service's address is not. An operator wanting the dashboard's
.I .onion
address must retrieve it separately, for example via
.BR aenigma-status (8).
.PP
The
.B aenigma-dashboard
onion service is configured with Tor client authorization for a
single authorized client whose name is identical to the service
name itself
.RB ( "aenigma-dashboard" ).
This is a fixed convention of this script, not a name chosen per
deployment or per operator.
.PP
Because each onion service is only (re-)configured when its
.I hostname
file is absent, running this script a second time after either
onion service has already started has no effect, even if the
underlying
.I torrc
entry, local address, port, or authorization has since changed by
other means. It is intended for initial setup, not as a general
reconciliation or repair command.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR aenigma-tor (8),
.BR aenigma-tor-auth (8),
.BR aenigma-status (8),
.BR aenigma-config (8),
.BR aenigma-keys (8),
.BR aenigma-start (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Romulus-Emanuel Ruja <romulus.ruja@aenigma.ro>
